<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/ux-research">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    UX research is the discipline\'s reality check: <strong>watching real people try to use the
    thing before shipping it</strong> — the evidence-based-practice culture of the medicine
    lesson, at product speed.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="decision-first questions, behavioural interviews, five users, triangulation" widget="equationStep" config={uxrCfg} />

  <h2>Methods and their honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surveys measure attitudes, poorly designed measure nothing:</strong> leading questions, response bias, unrepresentative samples (the statistics lesson\'s sampling pitfalls, questionnaire edition); the survey-research lesson of the sociology ladder is the deep treatment.</li>
    <li><strong>Analytics shows WHAT, never WHY:</strong> funnels and heatmaps locate friction (the data lesson\'s dashboards); the WHY needs a human watching a human — pair them.</li>
    <li><strong>Diary studies and field visits:</strong> context is invisible from the office — the anthropology lesson\'s ethnography, product edition: watch people in their own environment, where the workaround you never designed already exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Ethics and consent:</strong> participants deserve to know what they\'re recording and how data is used; research ops is the profession\'s institutional-review conscience (the ethics lesson, operationalised).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nielsen on usability heuristics and the 5-user finding; Bland & Osterwalder on testing value propositions; the data and anthropology lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
